Refactored Code Flow Overview

The refactored architecture introduces an event-driven workflow model that separates document ingestion, AI processing workflows, knowledge generation, and user interaction into independent platform components. This approach improves scalability, maintainability, and alignment with the enterprise data and AI platform architecture.

The processing pipeline begins when a document is uploaded through the CTB Workbench UI or received from upstream systems in future integrations. The document is processed by the CTB ingestion pipeline, which performs preprocessing and structural extraction. During this stage, the document is converted into a normalized format and divided into logical sections or chunks that can be processed independently by downstream AI workflows.

Once preprocessing is complete, the ingestion service publishes an event that triggers the workflow orchestration layer implemented using LangGraph. The orchestration service coordinates the execution of the AI processing workflows in a controlled sequence.

The first stage of AI processing is the Reauthor Workflow, which applies a set of LLM-powered agents to rewrite the document sections into a simplified and standardized format. The generated outputs are then passed to the Validation Workflow, where validation agents evaluate the reauthored content against the source document to detect potential information loss or inconsistencies.

Following validation, the outputs are presented to users through the CTB Workbench UI for review and approval. This human-in-the-loop step ensures that domain experts can verify the AI-generated results before they are committed to the knowledge platform.

Once a document is approved, an event is published to trigger the Knowledge Processing workflows. These workflows extract ontology-aligned artefacts from the approved content and generate vector embeddings for semantic retrieval. The extracted knowledge is then stored in the knowledge graph platform, while the embeddings are stored in the vector database for retrieval use cases.

Throughout this process, all platform components interact with storage systems, knowledge services, and metadata platforms through the MCP gateway, which provides governed and auditable access to shared platform resources.

This refactored flow enables the CTB platform to transition from a manual PoC execution model to an automated, event-driven architecture capable of supporting large-scale document processing and knowledge management workflows.

Conclusion

The proposed refactoring approach provides a structured path for transitioning the current PoC implementation into a production-ready architecture aligned with the Codify the Bank (CTB) platform strategy. By separating responsibilities across dedicated repositories and introducing an event-driven workflow model, the platform will move away from manual execution patterns toward a scalable and maintainable architecture.

The refactored structure enables clear separation between workflow orchestration, AI processing services, knowledge generation components, and platform governance capabilities, ensuring that each component can evolve independently as the programme expands. In addition, the introduction of centralized orchestration and governed platform access ensures improved operational visibility, traceability, and compliance with enterprise data and AI platform standards.

This restructuring also establishes the foundation required to support future phases of the CTB programme, including expanded document ingestion sources, additional AI agent workflows, enhanced knowledge graph capabilities, and more advanced retrieval use cases. By aligning the codebase with the target platform architecture early in the development lifecycle, the engineering team can ensure that the platform remains extensible, scalable, and maintainable as adoption grows across the organization.

The engineering refactoring backlog outlined in this document provides a prioritized set of implementation activities required to achieve this transition. These activities will guide the development team in progressively aligning the existing codebase with the target architecture while continuing to support the upcoming release objectives.


